{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-40923" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $-10a+\\frac{6b}{c}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fd891380468bf0a8f561952ac04ccfb4b58653fa1b03325957d5e07148877d4a"} {"question" "q-40923" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}90 & -30 \\\\ -30 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-27 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}90 & -30 & -27 \\\\ -30 & 10 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}90 & -30 & -27 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have  \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{10}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{10}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{10} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n-10a+\\frac{6b}{c}=-10\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{10})+\\frac{6\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3})}{1}=5.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fd891380468bf0a8f561952ac04ccfb4b58653fa1b03325957d5e07148877d4a"} {"question" "q-40923" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074648/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "be690e13415857adcd637bef036f10894ef79642118dca9bf20603ead96a82db"} {"question" "q-40923" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fd891380468bf0a8f561952ac04ccfb4b58653fa1b03325957d5e07148877d4a"} {"question" "q-40923" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "5"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "7"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{10}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fd891380468bf0a8f561952ac04ccfb4b58653fa1b03325957d5e07148877d4a"} {"question" "q-40923" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fd891380468bf0a8f561952ac04ccfb4b58653fa1b03325957d5e07148877d4a"} {"question" "q-40927" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 10\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $-2a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fd891380468bf0a8f561952ac04ccfb4b58653fa1b03325957d5e07148877d4a"} {"question" "q-40927" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}30 & -60 \\\\ -60 & 120\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-15 \\\\ 30\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -2 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}+2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\n Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n-2a+\\frac{b}{c}=-2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+\\frac{2}{1}=3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fd891380468bf0a8f561952ac04ccfb4b58653fa1b03325957d5e07148877d4a"} {"question" "q-40927" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074648/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "be690e13415857adcd637bef036f10894ef79642118dca9bf20603ead96a82db"} {"question" "q-40927" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fd891380468bf0a8f561952ac04ccfb4b58653fa1b03325957d5e07148877d4a"} {"question" "q-40927" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fd891380468bf0a8f561952ac04ccfb4b58653fa1b03325957d5e07148877d4a"} {"question" "q-40927" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fd891380468bf0a8f561952ac04ccfb4b58653fa1b03325957d5e07148877d4a"} {"question" "q-40941" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $6a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fd891380468bf0a8f561952ac04ccfb4b58653fa1b03325957d5e07148877d4a"} {"question" "q-40941" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 2 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 2 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}15 & 30 \\\\ 30 & 60\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}15 & 30 & 10 \\\\ 30 & 60 & 20\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}15 & 30 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}-2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n6a+\\frac{b}{c}=6\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{(-2)}{1}=2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fd891380468bf0a8f561952ac04ccfb4b58653fa1b03325957d5e07148877d4a"} {"question" "q-40941" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074648/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "be690e13415857adcd637bef036f10894ef79642118dca9bf20603ead96a82db"} {"question" "q-40941" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fd891380468bf0a8f561952ac04ccfb4b58653fa1b03325957d5e07148877d4a"} {"question" "q-40941" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fd891380468bf0a8f561952ac04ccfb4b58653fa1b03325957d5e07148877d4a"} {"question" "q-40941" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fd891380468bf0a8f561952ac04ccfb4b58653fa1b03325957d5e07148877d4a"} {"question" "q-137097" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-4 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $-9a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fd891380468bf0a8f561952ac04ccfb4b58653fa1b03325957d5e07148877d4a"} {"question" "q-137097" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}27 & -27 \\\\ -27 & 27\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}27 & -27 & -9 \\\\ -27 & 27 & 9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}27 & -27 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}+{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n-9a+\\frac{b}{c}=-9\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})+\\frac{1}{1}=4.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fd891380468bf0a8f561952ac04ccfb4b58653fa1b03325957d5e07148877d4a"} {"question" "q-137097" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074648/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "be690e13415857adcd637bef036f10894ef79642118dca9bf20603ead96a82db"} {"question" "q-137097" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fd891380468bf0a8f561952ac04ccfb4b58653fa1b03325957d5e07148877d4a"} {"question" "q-137097" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fd891380468bf0a8f561952ac04ccfb4b58653fa1b03325957d5e07148877d4a"} {"question" "q-137097" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fd891380468bf0a8f561952ac04ccfb4b58653fa1b03325957d5e07148877d4a"} {"question" "q-137131" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ {x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $3a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fd891380468bf0a8f561952ac04ccfb4b58653fa1b03325957d5e07148877d4a"} {"question" "q-137131" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -8\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -4 & -8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -4 & -8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6 & -24 \\\\ -24 & 96\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -32\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -24 & 8 \\\\ -24 & 96 & -32\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & -24 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{4}{3}+4{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3}+4{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n3a+\\frac{b}{c}=3\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{4}{1}=8.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fd891380468bf0a8f561952ac04ccfb4b58653fa1b03325957d5e07148877d4a"} {"question" "q-137131" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074648/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "be690e13415857adcd637bef036f10894ef79642118dca9bf20603ead96a82db"} {"question" "q-137131" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074648/state.json" "file_sha256" "fd891380468bf0a8f561952ac04ccfb4b58653fa1b03325957d5e07148877d4a"} {"question" "q-137131" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "5"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "7"] ["math" "8"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_c